1
00:01:31,595 --> 00:01:32,915
That was a long one.

2
00:01:37,804 --> 00:01:41,168
I don't suppose you can tell me
how it looks, can you?

3
00:01:42,363 --> 00:01:44,553
Unfortunately,
I'm just the technician.

4
00:01:45,436 --> 00:01:47,370
Your doctor will have
your results tomorrow.

5
00:01:49,780 --> 00:01:51,626
-Would you like some help to...
-Thank you very much.

6
00:01:52,388 --> 00:01:53,453
You're very kind.

7
00:02:05,213 --> 00:02:08,071
I mean, yeah, the
cancer has brought grief and pain

8
00:02:08,096 --> 00:02:11,023
and fear into my every
waking moment.

9
00:02:12,738 --> 00:02:15,538
Okay, Chris.
Today is a gift.

10
00:02:16,869 --> 00:02:19,475
That's why they call it
the present.

11
00:02:21,852 --> 00:02:22,953
Anyone else?

12
00:02:24,757 --> 00:02:25,970
Henry Kessler.

13
00:02:26,623 --> 00:02:27,765
Please.

14
00:02:28,718 --> 00:02:30,278
Stage four pancreatic.

15
00:02:31,071 --> 00:02:32,268
Terminal.

16
00:02:33,580 --> 00:02:35,694
That's what they tell me,
anyway, but...

17
00:02:39,616 --> 00:02:41,196
I'm gonna fight this
'til the end.

18
00:02:45,862 --> 00:02:47,415
How much time do I have?

19
00:02:51,045 --> 00:02:52,140
A year?

20
00:02:53,230 --> 00:02:54,883
Months at best.

21
00:02:56,346 --> 00:02:58,626
I still have a lot of work
that needs to be done.

22
00:02:59,438 --> 00:03:00,509
My advice?

23
00:03:02,063 --> 00:03:03,078
Retire.

24
00:03:04,816 --> 00:03:06,049
You know, at this stage,

25
00:03:07,346 --> 00:03:09,526
everyone always searches
for a way out.

26
00:03:10,599 --> 00:03:12,829
But sometimes,
those who accept their fate,

27
00:03:13,812 --> 00:03:14,996
have an easier time.

28
00:03:15,021 --> 00:03:16,541
Easier time? What?

29
00:03:18,168 --> 00:03:19,170
Dying?

30
00:03:22,062 --> 00:03:26,542
So, your advice to me
is to die easy?

31
00:04:41,373 --> 00:04:44,625
Help! Hey! Help! Help, please!

32
00:04:54,665 --> 00:04:56,396
I'd like to play a game.

33
00:04:57,539 --> 00:05:01,742
Perhaps one that addresses
those sticky fingers of yours.

34
00:05:03,159 --> 00:05:07,488
I've had my eyes on you
and I do not like what I see.

35
00:05:09,261 --> 00:05:12,245
Your job as a custodian
is a noble one.

36
00:05:12,868 --> 00:05:16,074
Sanitizing and sterilizing the
hospital...

37
00:05:16,099 --> 00:05:18,200
...helping patients
avoid sickness.

38
00:05:18,690 --> 00:05:23,246
But there is a sickness inside you
that needs to be excised.

39
00:05:23,555 --> 00:05:25,894
You have the ability
to not only save

40
00:05:25,919 --> 00:05:28,267
your soul today,
but your sight.

41
00:05:29,873 --> 00:05:32,079
All you have to do is
click the dial

42
00:05:32,170 --> 00:05:33,938
across the five positions

43
00:05:34,312 --> 00:05:36,505
and you will live to see
another day.

44
00:05:37,479 --> 00:05:38,902
You have 60 seconds.

45
00:05:39,686 --> 00:05:40,975
Help, please!

46
00:05:46,633 --> 00:05:50,145
Help me! Please, please,
please. Help. Help!

47
00:06:09,270 --> 00:06:11,622
I can't! I can't, I can't!

48
00:06:11,647 --> 00:06:14,150
Please, no!

49
00:06:24,752 --> 00:06:25,837
Fuck you!

50
00:07:08,900 --> 00:07:10,156
Good choice.

51
00:07:31,009 --> 00:07:32,224
Here you go, sir.

52
00:07:32,801 --> 00:07:34,055
Thank you.

53
00:07:47,966 --> 00:07:49,706
Can I get
a blueberry scone, please?

54
00:07:52,896 --> 00:07:54,149
Oh, they look good.

55
00:07:56,203 --> 00:07:57,218
Thank you.

56
00:08:01,535 --> 00:08:02,610
Henry.

57
00:08:05,244 --> 00:08:07,557
We were in the same
cancer support group.

58
00:08:08,478 --> 00:08:09,565
John Kramer.

59
00:08:09,972 --> 00:08:11,032
I remember you.

60
00:08:11,771 --> 00:08:13,288
Hey, good to see you.

61
00:08:15,288 --> 00:08:16,392
And you as well.

62
00:08:19,018 --> 00:08:20,038
You look marvelous.

63
00:08:20,691 --> 00:08:22,747
Thank you. I... I...
I feel great.

64
00:08:23,161 --> 00:08:25,428
Correct me if I'm wrong,
but you were, uh...

65
00:08:25,453 --> 00:08:27,846
Yeah, stage four pancreatic.

66
00:08:28,710 --> 00:08:30,233
But now, four months later,

67
00:08:30,868 --> 00:08:33,468
I'm in complete remission.

68
00:08:37,611 --> 00:08:38,730
How?

69
00:08:39,946 --> 00:08:42,390
It's a... it's a long story.

70
00:08:43,412 --> 00:08:45,432
I'd love to hear it.

71
00:08:46,657 --> 00:08:47,847
You got a minute to tell me?

72
00:08:48,962 --> 00:08:50,654
Sure. Sure.

73
00:08:56,604 --> 00:08:57,606
So, um,

74
00:08:58,385 --> 00:09:01,747
I read about a doctor,
a specialist of sorts.

75
00:09:01,771 --> 00:09:03,895
A Dr. Finn Pederson.

76
00:09:04,244 --> 00:09:06,464
He offered a treatment

77
00:09:06,983 --> 00:09:11,023
and it is a groundbreaking

78
00:09:11,608 --> 00:09:14,288
drug cocktail
combined with surgery.

79
00:09:20,328 --> 00:09:21,444
It's my war wound.

80
00:09:24,520 --> 00:09:26,949
I know it looks bad,

81
00:09:26,974 --> 00:09:29,674
but it was totally worth it.

82
00:09:30,580 --> 00:09:32,898
Yeah.

83
00:09:34,961 --> 00:09:37,371
-Is it available here?
-No.

84
00:09:38,089 --> 00:09:40,680
No, no, the treatment's not
available in the States.

85
00:09:40,705 --> 00:09:43,886
The, uh, the medication
isn't approved by the FDA.

86
00:09:44,901 --> 00:09:46,766
Hell, it isn't approved
by anybody.

87
00:09:48,396 --> 00:09:50,993
I, um... I haven't
seen them since...

88
00:09:51,994 --> 00:09:53,644
since my treatment in Norway.

89
00:09:54,692 --> 00:09:55,792
But...

90
00:10:01,195 --> 00:10:02,938
this link...

91
00:10:05,893 --> 00:10:06,911
is still good.

92
00:10:12,674 --> 00:10:13,748
Hey, John,

93
00:10:15,893 --> 00:10:17,500
good luck, man. Okay?

94
00:10:18,332 --> 00:10:20,505
I'm... I'm rooting for you.

95
00:10:22,042 --> 00:10:23,063
Thank you.

96
00:10:23,832 --> 00:10:24,936
Take care.

97
00:10:41,934 --> 00:10:44,195
Doctor, what exactly is
the procedure?

98
00:10:44,675 --> 00:10:47,998
Well, under the right circumstances
and with the proper stimuli,

99
00:10:48,300 --> 00:10:50,915
genes can be turned on and
turned off like a light switch.

100
00:10:51,480 --> 00:10:53,473
And you're targeting
the cancer cells?

101
00:10:53,533 --> 00:10:54,541
Exactly.

102
00:10:54,694 --> 00:10:58,303
What we've managed to do is to
create a new cutting-edge drug cocktail,

103
00:10:58,529 --> 00:10:59,679
along with surgery,

104
00:10:59,896 --> 00:11:02,242
to teach the malignant cells
to switch off.

105
00:11:02,512 --> 00:11:03,541
Fascinating.

106
00:11:04,299 --> 00:11:05,475
Hmm.

107
00:11:17,207 --> 00:11:21,294
My father, Dr. Finn Pederson,
was forced into hiding

108
00:11:21,633 --> 00:11:25,758
by corrupt governments and
their relationship to big pharma.

109
00:11:26,371 --> 00:11:27,379
Why?

110
00:11:28,325 --> 00:11:32,332
Because my father was on the
cusp of curing dozens of diseases.

111
00:11:32,751 --> 00:11:36,052
Which means no more need
for lifelong medication.

112
00:11:37,056 --> 00:11:39,136
Can you imagine
what that implies?

113
00:11:41,544 --> 00:11:43,572
Well, the big drug companies
certainly can.

114
00:11:44,485 --> 00:11:47,319
And I'm here to say
that this just cannot stand.

115
00:11:48,529 --> 00:11:52,372
So, while my father continues to make
further improvements to his formula,

116
00:11:53,141 --> 00:11:56,881
I'm taking his treatment
on the road for more trials,

117
00:11:57,512 --> 00:12:00,172
to save as many lives
as humanly possible.

118
00:12:33,534 --> 00:12:34,641
Who is this?

119
00:12:36,106 --> 00:12:37,548
This is Cecilia Pederson.

120
00:12:37,835 --> 00:12:38,981
Is this John Kramer?

121
00:12:39,877 --> 00:12:40,917
Yes, it is.

122
00:12:41,382 --> 00:12:42,387
Good.

123
00:12:42,956 --> 00:12:46,103
We received the email with
your medical records, Mr. Kramer.

124
00:12:46,300 --> 00:12:47,388
Thank you for reaching out.

125
00:12:47,682 --> 00:12:51,150
I'm sifting through potential
candidates for our program.

126
00:12:51,343 --> 00:12:52,344
So, I'm calling you now,

127
00:12:52,369 --> 00:12:55,109
because we have spots opening
for treatment in about three months.

128
00:13:00,718 --> 00:13:01,788
Mr. Kramer?

129
00:13:02,731 --> 00:13:03,953
Uh, yes, I'm here.

130
00:13:05,584 --> 00:13:07,027
You don't have three months,
do you?

131
00:13:09,749 --> 00:13:10,926
Okay, listen...

132
00:13:11,399 --> 00:13:13,249
I'm currently working in Mexico.

133
00:13:13,858 --> 00:13:15,678
Oslo has become
too difficult for us.

134
00:13:16,623 --> 00:13:18,599
But we have a team
of specialists here

135
00:13:18,807 --> 00:13:21,280
who share our belief
in the Pederson method

136
00:13:21,467 --> 00:13:23,963
and I've gathered
a group of eligible patients

137
00:13:24,160 --> 00:13:25,863
to start treatment
a week from now.

138
00:13:26,450 --> 00:13:28,457
I think I could add
one more spot.

139
00:13:29,215 --> 00:13:30,605
If you're interested.

140
00:13:31,573 --> 00:13:32,947
Yes, I'm interested.

141
00:13:33,880 --> 00:13:34,927
Henry Kessler...

142
00:13:35,213 --> 00:13:36,278
referred me to you.

143
00:13:36,303 --> 00:13:39,822
Henry. He's doing well.
I'm so pleased.

144
00:13:41,348 --> 00:13:42,350
I wonder if you could...

145
00:13:42,508 --> 00:13:44,951
could you tell me a little more
about the treatment?

146
00:13:45,057 --> 00:13:46,083
Of course.

147
00:13:46,300 --> 00:13:50,636
It's a two-prong treatment that
uses my father's Xaxiophenol cocktail.

148
00:13:50,790 --> 00:13:55,551
And in your case, it would be combined
with a cranial surgery to clear the tumor.

149
00:13:56,780 --> 00:13:59,466
When both are used together,
the results have been stunning.

150
00:13:59,638 --> 00:14:01,252
Over 90% success rate.

151
00:14:01,991 --> 00:14:03,153
I know it's a long trip,

152
00:14:03,265 --> 00:14:05,705
Mr. Kramer,
especially in your condition,

153
00:14:06,643 --> 00:14:09,936
but I want you to know that we
found a very beautiful location

154
00:14:09,961 --> 00:14:11,957
for our clinic
outside Mexico City.

155
00:14:12,689 --> 00:14:14,136
It's in a
well-off-the-grid area,

156
00:14:14,407 --> 00:14:18,150
in the countryside where you
can have safety and privacy.

157
00:14:18,978 --> 00:14:22,092
We'll also, of course, arrange
for a trusted driver at your arrival.

158
00:14:22,573 --> 00:14:23,903
What date did you have in mind?

159
00:14:24,831 --> 00:14:26,532
The 5th, Friday after next.

160
00:14:27,529 --> 00:14:28,742
I think I could make that.

161
00:14:45,381 --> 00:14:48,520
-J. Kramer? Yeah?
-Si.Yes.

162
00:14:48,607 --> 00:14:50,849
I'm Diego. Welcome to Mexico.

163
00:14:50,874 --> 00:14:52,905
Allow me, sir. Please. This way.

164
00:14:53,617 --> 00:14:56,357
There you go.

165
00:15:00,404 --> 00:15:01,629
Take a look at that.

166
00:15:01,654 --> 00:15:03,449
That's the Metropolitan
Cathedral.

167
00:15:04,064 --> 00:15:06,138
That's the revolutionary
memorial.

168
00:15:07,036 --> 00:15:09,124
That's the Angel
of Independence.

169
00:15:09,712 --> 00:15:12,386
Take a look at that.
Isn't it beautiful?

170
00:15:14,850 --> 00:15:15,871
Hey, J.

171
00:15:15,976 --> 00:15:19,638
That, my friend, that's the old
Aztec statue of Tlaloc.

172
00:15:20,334 --> 00:15:21,412
That's where the priests

173
00:15:21,437 --> 00:15:23,497
chop the hearts
out of their subjects

174
00:15:23,522 --> 00:15:24,976
and roll them down the stairs.

175
00:15:26,976 --> 00:15:29,470
Every day, a tourist wants me
to take them here.

176
00:15:29,495 --> 00:15:30,910
Very strange.

177
00:15:50,106 --> 00:15:51,180
Hey, J.

178
00:15:51,349 --> 00:15:52,460
You okay?

179
00:16:00,899 --> 00:16:01,958
Get out!

180
00:16:08,904 --> 00:16:09,983
What's your name?

181
00:16:10,883 --> 00:16:11,945
John Kramer.

182
00:16:12,309 --> 00:16:14,763
Lie to me again and I'll empty
this chamber in your eye.

183
00:16:15,866 --> 00:16:17,918
I'm not lying.
My name is John Kramer.

184
00:16:20,802 --> 00:16:22,953
Not to worry, senor
Kramer, you're fine.

185
00:16:23,343 --> 00:16:26,291
You're safe. Come with me.
Come, come.

186
00:16:26,773 --> 00:16:29,464
Here. Welcome. Watch your head.

187
00:16:55,429 --> 00:16:56,698
Your room.

188
00:16:57,180 --> 00:16:58,561
Here. Come.

189
00:17:01,560 --> 00:17:02,996
It's here. Good luck.

190
00:17:10,050 --> 00:17:11,176
Si?

191
00:17:13,286 --> 00:17:15,134
I am John Kramer.

192
00:17:16,759 --> 00:17:17,865
Come.

193
00:17:28,438 --> 00:17:30,139
I... Gabriela.

194
00:17:40,748 --> 00:17:42,063
Mi familia.

195
00:17:46,646 --> 00:17:47,801
Come.

196
00:17:56,331 --> 00:17:57,759
Sleep. Sleep here.

197
00:17:58,191 --> 00:17:59,891
Dr. Cecilia Pederson?

198
00:18:00,550 --> 00:18:01,607
Ah. Si.

199
00:18:03,306 --> 00:18:07,205
She saved life. My life.

200
00:18:10,271 --> 00:18:11,818
I get. I get now.

201
00:18:26,220 --> 00:18:29,037
Mr. Kramer, welcome.

202
00:18:34,615 --> 00:18:38,734
That was quite a reception
that I got on the way out here.

203
00:18:39,020 --> 00:18:40,322
Oh, our welcome wagon?

204
00:18:41,605 --> 00:18:42,988
I know, I'm sorry.

205
00:18:43,395 --> 00:18:45,944
But we've had to beef up
the security wherever we go.

206
00:18:47,154 --> 00:18:49,838
The big drug companies
are always one step behind

207
00:18:50,543 --> 00:18:53,924
and so we must keep our location
secret from everyone, even you.

208
00:18:55,493 --> 00:18:56,510
Come.

209
00:18:57,060 --> 00:18:58,557
I want you to meet the team.

210
00:19:07,378 --> 00:19:08,978
Yeah?

211
00:19:10,188 --> 00:19:13,633
That's Carlos.
Our local football star.

212
00:19:14,983 --> 00:19:16,245
His father is the caretaker.

213
00:19:39,757 --> 00:19:41,844
This used to be a
chemical factory back in the day.

214
00:20:19,116 --> 00:20:20,264
Mateo.

215
00:20:21,349 --> 00:20:22,467
Hello, Doctor.

216
00:20:23,135 --> 00:20:24,142
This is John Kramer.

217
00:20:24,415 --> 00:20:26,926
Very nice to meet
you, Mr. Kramer, and welcome to our clinic.

218
00:20:27,125 --> 00:20:28,453
You're in very good hands
with us.

219
00:20:29,231 --> 00:20:30,260
Hmm.

220
00:20:30,285 --> 00:20:33,251
Mateo is an anesthesiologist
at Hospital Alto Prado.

221
00:20:33,618 --> 00:20:37,677
He did his residency at Johns Hopkins
and has worked a lot with my father.

222
00:20:38,087 --> 00:20:39,564
-Yes.
-He'll be helping out

223
00:20:39,589 --> 00:20:40,727
with your surgery tomorrow.

224
00:20:41,073 --> 00:20:42,116
Thank you.

225
00:20:42,487 --> 00:20:44,297
-I'll be right here if you need anything.
-Thank you.

226
00:20:50,448 --> 00:20:52,799
This is Valentina,
one of our nurses.

227
00:20:53,236 --> 00:20:55,932
She'll be taking some scans
and drawing some blood,

228
00:20:56,111 --> 00:20:58,703
all in preparation for the
injection therapy later today.

229
00:20:59,722 --> 00:21:01,682
Mr. Sears' vitals look good.

230
00:21:01,947 --> 00:21:03,362
He's cleared for release.

231
00:21:03,590 --> 00:21:04,753
Wonderful.

232
00:21:06,437 --> 00:21:08,757
Parker Sears, John Kramer.

233
00:21:11,520 --> 00:21:13,714
Thyroid cancer, advanced. You?

234
00:21:14,619 --> 00:21:15,798
Uh, brain.

235
00:21:16,440 --> 00:21:18,627
Ah. You win.

236
00:21:23,220 --> 00:21:25,951
Sorry, they cut me open
yesterday.

237
00:21:26,983 --> 00:21:28,663
Took out a tumor
the size of my knob.

238
00:21:29,692 --> 00:21:31,051
I'm just about to start
the cocktail.

239
00:21:31,602 --> 00:21:33,327
Mutated natural killer cells.

240
00:21:33,718 --> 00:21:36,223
They're a type of
cytotoxic lymphocyte.

241
00:21:36,257 --> 00:21:37,668
Developed by my father.

242
00:21:39,250 --> 00:21:40,389
Good luck, John.

243
00:21:41,933 --> 00:21:42,967
Thank you.

244
00:21:44,118 --> 00:21:46,292
So, we've arranged
for you to stay at a hotel.

245
00:21:46,698 --> 00:21:47,730
Shall we begin?

246
00:21:54,488 --> 00:21:55,857
Please, don't move.

247
00:22:08,644 --> 00:22:10,532
So, Mr. Kramer...

248
00:22:11,157 --> 00:22:13,816
-What do you do? Like, for a living?
-John.

249
00:22:14,652 --> 00:22:16,062
Please, call me John.

250
00:22:16,791 --> 00:22:17,989
And what do you do, John?

251
00:22:18,734 --> 00:22:22,475
I've been a civil engineer and
an architect most of my life.

252
00:22:23,885 --> 00:22:25,025
Now, I...

253
00:22:26,422 --> 00:22:27,717
have a few hobbies.

254
00:22:28,632 --> 00:22:29,882
Hmm.

255
00:22:30,485 --> 00:22:31,632
Let me guess.

256
00:22:32,759 --> 00:22:34,067
Classic car renovation.

257
00:22:36,153 --> 00:22:38,067
- No?
- No.

258
00:22:38,270 --> 00:22:39,272
I, uh...

259
00:22:39,520 --> 00:22:42,216
I help people overcome
inner obstacles.

260
00:22:43,652 --> 00:22:45,899
Help them make positive
changes in their lives.

261
00:22:46,984 --> 00:22:48,581
Like a life coach, yeah?

262
00:22:49,956 --> 00:22:51,024
Something like that.

263
00:22:51,661 --> 00:22:52,762
Sounds nice.

264
00:22:54,024 --> 00:22:56,476
I can't think of anything more
important than helping others.

265
00:22:57,898 --> 00:22:58,960
Yeah.

266
00:23:01,499 --> 00:23:02,507
Tequila.

